<Policy title="How we publish and check the numbers" description="Understand The MoneyGoal’s AI-assisted publishing, source checks, calculation methods, automatic holds and the limitations of automated financial education.">
<h2>Evidence before a draft</h2><p>We use specific regulator, government, industry-association and issuer documents. Each article identifies the market and links to its sources. An institution’s homepage is not evidence for a detailed claim. Publication, effective and access dates serve different purposes.</p>
<h2>AI assistance and accountability</h2><p>AI helps draft and separately checks statements against retrieved evidence. Code validates financial examples and publication rules. These checks do not establish expert approval and cannot guarantee accuracy. We do not fabricate authors, qualifications, interviews, product tests or personal experience.</p>
<h2>When we hold an article</h2><p>Missing evidence, conflicting statements, stale product terms, unavailable sources, unapproved promotions and failed build checks stop publication. We publish fewer articles when necessary rather than fill a quota. Country eligibility and advertiser approval must be recorded before an offer is activated.</p>
<h2>Calculations</h2><p>Our models are simplified educational scenarios. They cannot capture future returns, all lender conventions, taxes or individual circumstances. Results change with assumptions, not with affiliate commission. Inputs remain in your browser.</p>
{Object.entries(calculations).map(([id, make]) => <section id={id}><h3>{id.replaceAll('-', ' ')}</h3><p>{make().assumptions}</p></section>)}
<h3 id="sip-method">Monthly investing</h3><p>With monthly contribution P, monthly rate i and n months, the end-of-month value is P × ((1+i)^n − 1) / i. Beginning-of-month contributions multiply that result by (1+i). At zero return the result is P × n. Nominal annual rates are divided by twelve; effective annual rates use a different conversion. Subtracting an annual expense ratio from gross return is an approximation, not an exact simulation of daily NAV deductions. Published fund returns may already include expenses: subtracting fees again would count them twice.</p>
<h3 id="debt-method">Debt repayment</h3><p>We accrue interest at APR divided by twelve, pay each fixed minimum, and direct the remaining fixed budget by highest APR (avalanche) or smallest balance (snowball). Freed payments stay in the budget. We stop at payoff or 1,200 months and explicitly show when debts do not clear. Actual daily interest, changing rates, fees and minimum-payment rules can alter results.</p>
<h3 id="card-method">Card annual fees</h3><p>Extra rewards equal capped fee-card rewards minus rewards on the same eligible spending with the alternative. We subtract the annual fee, entered interest and other fees. A cap can mean there is no break-even point or that benefits fall again at higher spend. Welcome bonuses, taxes, perk valuations and changing redemption values are excluded.</p>
<h2>Updates and corrections</h2><p>Evergreen articles are queued for review at least every ninety days. Product terms are checked more frequently. Overdue pages show a review notice and stop showing commercial offers. <a href="/corrections">Report a correction</a>.</p>
</Policy>
